from c8e_base import *
LB(8,4,'Language work: farm tools, synonyms from context, archaic words, silent letters, harvest dish talk and slogans','Language work for Harvest Hymn: farm tools, new words, silent letters, a talk and a slogan','Words from the harvest field',
 "A harvest hymn thanks the earth and the farmer. The language work gives us the names of farm tools, words from older English, silent letters that we write but do not say, a way to give a group talk and a way to write a short slogan.",
 [("Farm tools have special names, and each name tells the job. A rake has a long handle with metal teeth and collects hay or leaves. A trowel is a small hand tool for digging and making shallow furrows.","rake, trowel"),
  ("A sickle is a hand tool with a curved blade and a short handle, and it is used to harvest crops. A scythe has a long curved blade on a long handle and cuts tall grass. A plough is pulled by oxen and turns over the soil so that seeds can be sown. A spade has a flat rectangular blade on a long wooden handle and is used to dig the soil.","sickle, scythe, plough, spade"),
  ("When a word is new, we can guess its meaning from the words around it, called context clues. In 'The king was beneficent to the poor because he took good care of them', the reason after because shows that beneficent means kind.","context clues, beneficent"),
  ("Munificent means very generous, as in a person who donates a lot to the needy. Succour means help given in trouble, as in friends supporting someone who has lost a job.","munificent, succour"),
  ("Radiance means brightness, as when the sun's rays fill a dark room. Ineffable means too great to be described in words, as in the ineffable joy of a child with a gift.","radiance, ineffable"),
  ("Archaic words are old words that were once common but are now used mostly in poems and old books. Morrow means tomorrow, hither means to this place, quoth means said, betwixt means between, and yonder means over there.","archaic words: morrow, hither, quoth, betwixt, yonder"),
  ("In English some letters are written but not pronounced, and these are called silent letters. In knife, know and knowledge, the k is silent.","silent letters, silent k"),
  ("The letter n is silent when it comes after m at the end of a word, as in hymn, column, autumn and solemn. The letter b is silent when it comes after m, as in womb, tomb, bomb, climb and comb. Say these words aloud and notice that the last letter makes no sound.","silent n after m, silent b after m"),
  ("For the listening task you hear a talk on paddy cultivation. Listen for the steps and number them in order, then listen again to answer questions on the main step of preparing the soil, how long seeds take to grow into seedlings and why grains are dried after threshing.","listening to a talk and ordering events"),
  ("Steps in growing rice include ploughing, puddling, transplanting seedlings, irrigating, harvesting, threshing and milling. Puddling means working the wet soil into a soft mud before planting seedlings. Confirm the exact order from the talk when you listen.","paddy cultivation steps"),
  ("For the speaking task, work in a group and present a popular harvest dish of your region. Cover its name and other names, its ingredients, how it is cooked and served, its taste, any special ritual and a memory linked to it, and let every member speak.","group presentation on a harvest dish"),
  ("A good presentation has a clear order. Greet the audience, introduce your group and topic, explain the structure, speak about each point with linking phrases such as Let us begin by, Next, and I would now like to discuss, then conclude with To summarise and thank the audience.","presentation structure"),
  ("A slogan is a short, catchy sentence that spreads a message. It focuses on one main idea, uses simple language and is brief. Examples are 'Learn, Grow, Succeed' and 'Go Green by Keeping the Planet Clean'.","slogan"),
  ("To write a slogan for the school notice board to thank farmers, choose the main idea, which is gratitude for food, and say it in a few words, for example 'Thank a farmer, thank your plate'. Then write it on chart paper and decorate it.","writing a slogan"),
  ("Sample slogan checks: Is it short? Is the message clear? Is the language simple? Does it catch attention with rhythm or a play on words? If yes to all four, the slogan is ready.","slogan checks")],
 ("Match each tool with its job and say why the harvest word 'sickle' is used for crops but 'scythe' for grass. Then name the silent letter in 'climb'.",
  [("A sickle has a short handle and a curved blade for cutting crops, and a scythe has a long handle and a long curved blade for tall grass.","The handle and blade decide the job."),
   ("In 'climb', the letter b is silent because it follows m.","Silent b after m."),
   ("Both tools cut, but the length of the blade and handle shows the kind of plant.","Compare the two tools.")]),
 ("Say what an archaic word is, give two examples with their present-day meanings, and give one rule for silent letters.",
  ["old words now used mostly in poems and old books","morrow means tomorrow and hither means to this place","the n after m at the end is silent, as in hymn, or the b after m is silent, as in comb"],
  ["Pronouncing the b in comb","Thinking archaic words are made-up words"]),
 ["Rake, trowel, sickle, scythe, plough and spade are farm tools with different jobs.","Context clues help us guess a word's meaning.","Archaic words: morrow, hither, quoth, betwixt, yonder.","Silent n after m (hymn) and silent b after m (comb).","A slogan is short, simple and gives one clear message."],
 [mcq("v4b-1",1,"Which tool is pulled by oxen to turn over the soil?","plough",[("rake","A rake collects hay or leaves."),("trowel","A trowel is a small digging tool.")],"The plough turns over soil for sowing."),
  mcq("v4b-2",1,"A tool with a long handle and metal teeth for collecting leaves is a","rake",[("spade","A spade has a flat blade for digging."),("sickle","A sickle has a curved blade.")],"A rake has metal teeth."),
  mcq("v4b-3",1,"A scythe is used to","cut tall grass",[("dig shallow furrows","That is a trowel."),("turn over soil","That is a plough.")],"A scythe has a long curved blade."),
  mcq("v4b-4",1,"'Munificent' means","very generous",[("very bright","That is radiant."),("very sad","Not the meaning.")],"A munificent person gives a lot."),
  mcq("v4b-5",1,"'Succour' means","help in trouble",[("brightness","That is radiance."),("anger","Not the meaning.")],"Succour is help."),
  mcq("v4b-6",1,"'Ineffable joy' is joy that is","too great to put into words",[("small and short","Not the meaning."),("hidden from others","Not the meaning.")],"Ineffable means indescribable."),
  mcq("v4b-7",2,"The archaic word 'morrow' means","tomorrow",[("yesterday","This is the opposite."),("morning","It looks similar but is not the meaning.")],"Morrow is the next day."),
  mcq("v4b-8",2,"The archaic word 'betwixt' means","between",[("above","Not the meaning."),("beyond","Not the meaning.")],"Betwixt is an old form of between."),
  mcq("v4b-9",2,"Which word contains a silent 'n'?","autumn",[("tune","Here n is pronounced."),("sunny","Here n is pronounced.")],"After m, the n is silent."),
  mcq("v4b-10",2,"Which word contains a silent 'b'?","comb",[("bomb-shell","The first b is pronounced here."),("blue","The b is pronounced.")],"B after m is silent in comb."),
  tf("v4b-11",2,"In the word 'know', the letter k is pronounced.",False,"The k is silent in know."),
  mcq("v4b-12",3,"A good slogan is","short, simple and focused on one message",[("long with many ideas","A slogan should be brief."),("full of difficult words","It should use simple language.")],"Brevity and a clear message make a slogan work."),
  mcq("v4b-13",3,"In a group presentation, which comes just before the conclusion?","describing the details of each point",[("greeting the audience","That comes first."),("introducing the members","That comes near the start.")],"The details come before the conclusion.")])
LB(8,4,'Language work: weather and farm words, homophones, words in context, collocations, present tenses, determiners, role play and a formal letter','Language work for Waiting for the Rain: homophones, present tenses, determiners and a formal letter','Rain words and rain letters',
 "In Waiting for the Rain, a farming family waits for the monsoon. The language work gives us weather and farm words, words that sound alike, tenses for the present, small words called determiners, a role play and a formal letter.",
 [("The text has many weather and farm words. A gentle wind is a breeze, rumbling sound with rain is thunder, land for crops is a field, to turn over the field is to plough, to put seeds in the soil is to sow, and collected crops are the harvest.","breeze, thunder, field, plough, sow, harvest"),
  ("Homophones are words that sound the same but differ in spelling and meaning. Piece and peace, weather and whether, son and sun, and rain and reign are homophones from this chapter.","homophones: piece/peace, weather/whether, son/sun, rain/reign"),
  ("Weather is the condition of the sky and air, but whether is used for a choice or doubt: 'He wondered whether it would rain'. A piece is a part, but peace is calm.","weather/whether, piece/peace"),
  ("Plain means flat or simple, but a plane is an aircraft. To sell is to exchange for money, but a cell is a tiny unit of life or a room. To seem is to appear, but a seam is a line where cloth is joined.","plain/plane, sell/cell, seem/seam"),
  ("Always choose the homophone by its meaning in the sentence. 'The farmer looked at the sky and wondered whether it would rain' uses whether because he is in doubt.","choose by meaning"),
  ("We can find a word's meaning from the sentence around it. 'Being annoyed, she muttered something nobody heard' shows that muttered means spoke in a low voice. 'Felt forlorn for a week' means sad and neglected.","muttered, forlorn"),
  ("'At a loss' means confused and not knowing what to do. 'Her eyes sparkled' means her eyes showed joy.","at a loss, sparkled"),
  ("A collocation is a pair of words that naturally go together. We say favourable weather, wrinkled skin, dejected expression, fragrant garden, ceaseless activity and beloved country. Changing a word makes it sound odd, as in a wrinkled garden.","collocations"),
  ("The simple present tense states facts, habits and general truths: 'It is hard not to be able to work' and 'I am too old'. It uses the first form of the verb, with s or es for he, she and it.","simple present tense"),
  ("The present progressive tense shows an action happening now, made with am, is or are and the -ing form: 'What are you smiling at?' and 'That is what I am talking about'.","present progressive tense"),
  ("The present perfect tense links the past with the present, made with has or have and the third form of the verb: 'The earth has worked for years' and 'I have worked hard and honestly'. It is used when the result or the time period still matters now.","present perfect tense"),
  ("A determiner is a word that comes before a noun to tell which one or how many, without describing it. Articles are a, an and the. Demonstratives are this, that, these and those. Possessives are my, our, your, his, her, its and their.","determiners"),
  ("Numeral determiners give quantity. Definite ones are one, two, three. Indefinite ones are some, any, no, all, much, many, few, little, several and less. Use much with uncountable nouns and many with countable nouns, and some in statements but any in negatives and questions.","numeral determiners"),
  ("Few means not many and little means not much, with a negative feeling. A few and a little mean some, with a positive feeling. Distributive determiners are each, every, either and neither. Either means one of two and neither means not one of the two.","few and a few, distributive determiners"),
  ("For the speaking task, play the roles of Rain and Earth meeting after a long dry spell. Rain asks how the Earth has been, and Earth shows relief and gratitude and explains why rain matters to farms, plants and animals.","role play: Rain and Earth"),
  ("A formal letter to the Municipal Councillor asking for a rainwater harvesting unit has the sender's address, date, the receiver's designation and address, a subject line, a salutation, four paragraphs, a closing and the sender's name. Paragraph one draws attention to the problem, two gives causes and consequences, three makes the request with suggestions, and four concludes.","formal letter format"),
  ("Use formal language, short sentences, and a clear purpose in the letter, and give relevant details such as the ward number. End with 'Yours sincerely' and sign.","formal letter tips")],
 ("Pick the correct word and explain: 'The farmer wondered (weather/whether) the rains would come.' Then fill in with a determiner: '___ of the two roads leads to the river' (either/every).",
  [("The correct word is whether, because it expresses doubt about a choice.","Weather is the condition of the sky."),
   ("The correct determiner is either, because it means one of two.","Every refers to all, not one of two.")]),
 ("Explain the simple present, the present progressive and the present perfect tense with one example of each.",
  ["simple present for facts and habits, such as The farmer ploughs the field","present progressive for an action now, such as The rain is falling","present perfect links past and present, such as The earth has worked for years"],
  ["Using the -ing form without am, is or are","Using the simple past for the present perfect"]),
 ["Homophones sound alike but differ in meaning: weather/whether, piece/peace.","Collocations are natural word pairs such as favourable weather.","Simple present for facts, present progressive for now, present perfect for past linked to now.","Determiners come before a noun: articles, demonstratives, possessives and numerals.","A formal letter has an address, date, subject, four paragraphs and a close."],
 [mcq("v4c-1",1,"Which spelling fits: 'He wondered ___ it would rain.'","whether",[("weather","That is the condition of the sky."),("wether","Not the right word here.")],"Whether shows doubt."),
  mcq("v4c-2",1,"'Piece' and 'peace' are","homophones",[("synonyms","They have different meanings."),("antonyms","They are not opposites.")],"They sound alike but differ in spelling and meaning."),
  mcq("v4c-3",1,"Which word means 'calm'?","peace",[("piece","That is a part."),("pace","That means speed.")],"Peace is calm."),
  mcq("v4c-4",1,"'Felt forlorn' means","sad and neglected",[("angry and bitter","Not the meaning."),("upset and irritated","Not the meaning.")],"Forlorn means sad and lonely."),
  mcq("v4c-5",1,"Which collocation is natural?","favourable weather",[("fragrant skin","Fragrant goes with garden."),("wrinkled garden","Wrinkled goes with skin.")],"Favourable goes with weather."),
  mcq("v4c-6",2,"'What are you smiling at?' is in the","present progressive tense",[("simple present tense","Simple present would be 'What do you smile at?'"),("present perfect tense","That would use have or has.")],"Are plus -ing."),
  mcq("v4c-7",2,"'The earth has worked for years' is in the","present perfect tense",[("simple present tense","It uses has and a third form."),("present progressive tense","It does not use am, is or are plus -ing.")],"Has plus the third form."),
  mcq("v4c-8",2,"Choose the correct form: 'Ravi ___ (see) improvement since he adopted crop rotation.'","has seen",[("sees","That is simple present."),("is seeing","That is present progressive.")],"Since gives present perfect."),
  mcq("v4c-9",2,"Which is a possessive determiner?","their",[("these","That is a demonstrative."),("some","That is a numeral determiner.")],"Their shows ownership."),
  mcq("v4c-10",2,"'Many' is used with","countable nouns",[("uncountable nouns","Much is used with these."),("verbs","Determiners come before nouns.")],"Many goes with countable nouns like snails."),
  mcq("v4c-11",2,"'Neither' means","not one of the two",[("one of the two","That is either."),("all of them","That is all.")],"Neither means none of two."),
  mcq("v4c-12",3,"Which is the correct meaning of 'a few'?","some, positive",[("almost none, negative","That is few."),("a large number","That is many.")],"A few means some."),
  mcq("v4c-13",3,"In the letter to the Councillor, paragraph three should","make the request and give suggestions",[("describe the problem","That is paragraph one."),("conclude the letter","That is paragraph four.")],"Paragraph three makes the request.")])
